WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.318
Welcome to Module 2 of the Azure
Security Engineering learning path:

2
00:00:04.318 --> 00:00:07.462
DDoS protection and the
web application firewall.

3
00:00:07.462 --> 00:00:10.313
In Module 1 you built
the exposure register.

4
00:00:10.313 --> 00:00:14.433
In this module you take every public
I P in it to DDoS protection,

5
00:00:14.433 --> 00:00:17.841
with logs and an alert, and
every internet-facing web

6
00:00:17.841 --> 00:00:21.724
endpoint to a web application
firewall in prevention mode.

7
00:00:21.724 --> 00:00:26.854
It covers Steps 3 and 4 of the Azure
Security Engineering implementation guide,

8
00:00:26.854 --> 00:00:31.720
and takes about fifty minutes, plus
a lab in your own test subscription.

9
00:00:35.470 --> 00:00:36.395
Why both?

10
00:00:36.395 --> 00:00:40.802
Learn's overview, What is Azure DDoS
Protection, says it works at layers

11
00:00:40.802 --> 00:00:45.133
three and four, and that layer seven
needs a web application firewall.

12
00:00:45.133 --> 00:00:48.108
So an internet-facing workload needs both.

13
00:00:48.108 --> 00:00:52.670
And each has to be in the mode that
acts, not only the mode that logs.

14
00:00:52.670 --> 00:00:57.723
Learn's Zero Trust recommendations for
the Application Gateway web application

15
00:00:57.723 --> 00:01:01.587
firewall put it plainly: in
detection mode it logs and forwards;

16
00:01:01.587 --> 00:01:03.370
only prevention mode blocks.

17
00:01:03.370 --> 00:01:08.157
A firewall left in detection mode is the
first danger in the guide's dangers table.

18
00:01:08.157 --> 00:01:13.459
So this module takes every public I P in
the register to DDoS protection with logs

19
00:01:13.459 --> 00:01:17.891
and an alert, and every web endpoint
to a firewall in prevention mode,

20
00:01:17.891 --> 00:01:21.420
tuned first so it doesn't
block legitimate users.

21
00:01:25.170 --> 00:01:28.083
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module.

22
00:01:28.083 --> 00:01:30.865
You'll say what DDoS
infrastructure protection,

23
00:01:30.865 --> 00:01:35.595
I P Protection and Network Protection each
give, and what neither paid tier covers.

24
00:01:35.595 --> 00:01:40.157
You'll check that every public I P
is protected, logged and alerting.

25
00:01:40.157 --> 00:01:44.443
You'll explain why a simulation runs
only through an approved partner,

26
00:01:44.443 --> 00:01:46.882
and only against your own protected I Ps.

27
00:01:46.882 --> 00:01:51.400
You'll take a web application firewall
policy from detection to prevention,

28
00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:55.345
with request body inspection, the
current rule set and a rate limit.

29
00:01:55.345 --> 00:01:57.617
And you'll prove it blocks a test request,

30
00:01:57.617 --> 00:02:00.370
and roll it back if it
blocks legitimate traffic.

31
00:02:00.370 --> 00:02:04.801
You need Module 1 and its public I P
query, and the guide's decision record

32
00:02:04.801 --> 00:02:09.750
template, because the DDoS tier and
the Front Door tier are recorded there.

33
00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:15.750
Unit 1 is the guide's Step 3.

34
00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:18.750
Learn's tier comparison is
the source for this table.

35
00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:23.300
Every public I P in Azure gets
infrastructure protection at no cost.

36
00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:28.830
I P Protection, billed per protected I P,
and Network Protection, billed per plan,

37
00:02:28.830 --> 00:02:34.700
both add adaptive tuning, attack analytics,
metrics, alerts and mitigation reports.

38
00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:38.504
Network Protection also adds
the DDoS Rapid Response team,

39
00:02:38.504 --> 00:02:42.230
cost protection and a web
application firewall discount,

40
00:02:42.230 --> 00:02:46.112
and it protects Basic public I
Ps, which I P Protection doesn't.

41
00:02:46.112 --> 00:02:51.350
Learn's cost optimization guidance suggests
Network Protection when many public I

42
00:02:51.350 --> 00:02:56.363
Ps across subscriptions need coverage, and
I P Protection for a few critical ones.

43
00:02:56.363 --> 00:02:57.375
Two limits.

44
00:02:57.375 --> 00:03:01.058
Neither covers a public I P
attached to a NAT gateway;

45
00:03:01.058 --> 00:03:03.713
the guide lists those as an exclusion.

46
00:03:03.713 --> 00:03:06.538
And both work at layers
three and four only.

47
00:03:06.538 --> 00:03:10.130
Whichever you choose, record
it in the decision record.

48
00:03:13.880 --> 00:03:18.280
Here are the guide's Step 3 commands,
from Learn's DDoS quickstarts.

49
00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:22.647
Create one Network Protection plan
in the connectivity subscription,

50
00:03:22.647 --> 00:03:25.830
and link each virtual network
that holds public I Ps.

51
00:03:25.830 --> 00:03:30.154
Or, for I P Protection, enable
it on one Standard public I P.

52
00:03:30.154 --> 00:03:35.605
Turn on diagnostic logs for each
protected public I P, to the workspace.

53
00:03:35.605 --> 00:03:37.043
Then alert on attack.

54
00:03:37.043 --> 00:03:41.667
The metric called Under DDoS attack
or not reads one during mitigation,

55
00:03:41.667 --> 00:03:45.117
and Learn's tutorial alerts
at a maximum of one or more.

56
00:03:45.117 --> 00:03:47.281
One caution from Learn's quickstart:

57
00:03:47.281 --> 00:03:52.112
a virtual network with DDoS protection
can't be moved between resource groups or

58
00:03:52.112 --> 00:03:56.943
subscriptions, and disabling protection
to move it resets the tuned thresholds.

59
00:03:56.943 --> 00:03:59.917
Then hold it with policy
in Step 9: Learn's page,

60
00:03:59.917 --> 00:04:04.901
Secure your Azure DDoS Protection
deployment, names the built-in policies,

61
00:04:04.901 --> 00:04:09.242
one that protects virtual networks
with a Modify or Audit effect,

62
00:04:09.242 --> 00:04:12.570
and one that requires
the DDoS resource logs.

63
00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:18.394
The response has to be exercised,

64
00:04:18.394 --> 00:04:22.695
and Learn sets the rules in its
tutorial on DDoS simulation testing.

65
00:04:22.695 --> 00:04:27.559
Simulations run only through
Microsoft's approved partners, MazeBolt,

66
00:04:27.559 --> 00:04:33.000
Red Button and RedWolf, only against the
agency's own protected Azure public I Ps,

67
00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:36.133
and preferably in staging
or off-peak windows.

68
00:04:36.133 --> 00:04:40.570
Before the exercise, write the response
team and runbook that Learn's page on

69
00:04:40.570 --> 00:04:44.865
securing a DDoS deployment recommends,
including how to raise a severity

70
00:04:44.865 --> 00:04:49.020
A support request to the Rapid Response
team under Network Protection.

71
00:04:49.020 --> 00:04:53.862
The exercise proves three things the
guide's Validate step asks for: the alert

72
00:04:53.862 --> 00:04:57.039
fired, the action group
paged the security monitor,

73
00:04:57.039 --> 00:04:59.082
and the logs show the mitigation.

74
00:04:59.082 --> 00:05:03.577
The rollout itself is gentle:
one non-production network first,

75
00:05:03.577 --> 00:05:07.985
the policy in audit for an evaluation
cycle, then rings, the hub,

76
00:05:07.985 --> 00:05:13.710
then Online and Corp landing zones, and
only then the policy's Modify effect.

77
00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:21.522
Unit 2 is the guide's Step 4,
the web application firewall.

78
00:05:21.522 --> 00:05:26.078
Azure W A F runs in two places:
on Front Door, which is global,

79
00:05:26.078 --> 00:05:31.022
and on Application Gateway W A F
version two, which is regional.

80
00:05:31.022 --> 00:05:35.173
Either way it's a policy of managed
rules, custom rules and settings.

81
00:05:35.173 --> 00:05:37.960
Front Door's managed rules
need the Premium tier.

82
00:05:37.960 --> 00:05:43.347
Microsoft's Azure network security Zero
Trust recommendations check the same things

83
00:05:43.347 --> 00:05:48.135
on both: prevention mode, request body
inspection, the default rule set, the bot

84
00:05:48.135 --> 00:05:53.298
protection rule set, rate limiting, a
JavaScript challenge, and diagnostic logging.

85
00:05:53.298 --> 00:05:57.005
Application Gateway adds
the H T T P DDoS rule set;

86
00:05:57.005 --> 00:05:59.785
Front Door adds a CAPTCHA challenge.

87
00:05:59.785 --> 00:06:02.994
The Zero Trust Assessment
automates these checks,

88
00:06:02.994 --> 00:06:06.330
so a policy that misses
one shows up as a finding.

89
00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:13.473
Here are the guide's
Application Gateway commands,

90
00:06:13.473 --> 00:06:15.655
from Learn's Azure C L I reference.

91
00:06:15.655 --> 00:06:20.130
Create the policy in detection mode,
with request body inspection on.

92
00:06:20.130 --> 00:06:24.110
The C L I's default rule set is
version two point one; Learn's page,

93
00:06:24.110 --> 00:06:28.169
Secure your Azure Application
Gateway, recommends the latest,

94
00:06:28.169 --> 00:06:32.930
two point two at the guide's reading,
validated in log mode before blocking.

95
00:06:32.930 --> 00:06:34.980
So name the version you've validated.

96
00:06:34.980 --> 00:06:39.127
Add the bot protection rule set, in
commercial Azure, and a rate limit

97
00:06:39.127 --> 00:06:43.792
grouped by client address on the login
path, from Learn's rate limit examples.

98
00:06:43.792 --> 00:06:47.315
Then tune: detection for at
least one full business cycle,

99
00:06:47.315 --> 00:06:51.735
two weeks by the program's default,
reading the firewall log for matches

100
00:06:51.735 --> 00:06:56.231
on legitimate traffic, and scoping
each exception to the narrowest rule.

101
00:06:56.231 --> 00:06:59.505
Then switch to prevention,
one application at a time.

102
00:06:59.505 --> 00:07:02.935
Rollback is the same command
with the mode back to detection,

103
00:07:02.935 --> 00:07:05.405
which Learn documents for false positives.

104
00:07:05.405 --> 00:07:10.500
The guide also gives the policy as
Bicep, from Azure Verified Modules.

105
00:07:14.250 --> 00:07:15.562
Where are agencies?

106
00:07:15.562 --> 00:07:18.661
In commercial Azure,
which a G C C tenant uses,

107
00:07:18.661 --> 00:07:21.501
everything in this module is available.

108
00:07:21.501 --> 00:07:24.712
The guide's availability
table says what to plan for.

109
00:07:24.712 --> 00:07:28.804
The Application Gateway firewall's
detection mode doesn't block,

110
00:07:28.804 --> 00:07:30.699
so Step 4 runs prevention mode.

111
00:07:30.699 --> 00:07:33.900
Front Door's managed rule
sets need the Premium tier.

112
00:07:33.900 --> 00:07:39.106
And DDoS Network Protection is billed per
plan, I P Protection per protected I P,

113
00:07:39.106 --> 00:07:42.950
with simulations only through
Learn's approved partners.

114
00:07:42.950 --> 00:07:49.271
On other clouds, the guide names AWS
Shield and AWS W A F, Google Cloud Armor,

115
00:07:49.271 --> 00:07:54.750
and O C I W A F as a regional policy
on a load balancer or at the edge.

116
00:07:54.750 --> 00:07:59.430
DDoS protection on Oracle Cloud
Infrastructure isn't compared there.

117
00:08:03.180 --> 00:08:06.539
The lab runs the guide's Step
3 and Step 4 checks in a test

118
00:08:06.539 --> 00:08:11.167
subscription where someone has applied
those steps to a test application.

119
00:08:11.167 --> 00:08:14.367
Nothing in Azure changes, and
the authors haven't run it.

120
00:08:14.367 --> 00:08:18.796
You need a DDoS Network Protection
plan linked to a test virtual network,

121
00:08:18.796 --> 00:08:23.076
a protected Standard public I P with
its diagnostic setting and alert,

122
00:08:23.076 --> 00:08:28.168
and a test application behind an Application
Gateway firewall in prevention mode.

123
00:08:28.168 --> 00:08:31.780
Step one lists every
public I P's DDoS state.

124
00:08:31.780 --> 00:08:35.158
The guide's Validate step says
each should show Enabled,

125
00:08:35.158 --> 00:08:38.168
or Virtual Network Inherited
in a linked network.

126
00:08:38.168 --> 00:08:41.289
Step two exports the plan
and its linked networks,

127
00:08:41.289 --> 00:08:44.030
which the guide's
evidence checklist names.

128
00:08:44.030 --> 00:08:47.043
Step three checks the
protected I P's diagnostic

129
00:08:47.043 --> 00:08:50.055
setting and the alert on
the under-attack metric.

130
00:08:50.055 --> 00:08:52.276
The simulation isn't part of the lab:

131
00:08:52.276 --> 00:08:55.770
Learn allows it only
through an approved partner.

132
00:08:59.520 --> 00:09:03.838
Step four checks the policy, with
the guide's own Validate query:

133
00:09:03.838 --> 00:09:07.657
mode, request body inspection
and the managed rule sets.

134
00:09:07.657 --> 00:09:10.995
Expect Prevention,
true, and the rule sets.

135
00:09:10.995 --> 00:09:14.628
Step five sends a test
request carrying a benign SQL

136
00:09:14.628 --> 00:09:17.945
injection string to a path
on the test application.

137
00:09:17.945 --> 00:09:23.045
That request is the path's; the guide says
to send one and expect a four oh three.

138
00:09:23.045 --> 00:09:26.632
Step six finds the matching
row in the firewall log.

139
00:09:26.632 --> 00:09:31.936
The guide's Step 4 sends the log to
resource-specific tables, and Learn's page,

140
00:09:31.936 --> 00:09:34.929
Diagnostic logs for Application Gateway,

141
00:09:34.929 --> 00:09:38.008
names the firewall table
A G W Firewall Logs.

142
00:09:38.008 --> 00:09:42.936
The query command comes from the
Azure C L I's log analytics extension,

143
00:09:42.936 --> 00:09:46.739
and takes the workspace's I D,
a GUID, not its resource I D.

144
00:09:46.739 --> 00:09:49.333
Logs can take a few minutes to arrive.

145
00:09:49.333 --> 00:09:53.232
Step seven: nothing changed,
so there's nothing to undo.

146
00:09:53.232 --> 00:09:58.085
If step five returned anything but four
oh three, the firewall isn't blocking:

147
00:09:58.085 --> 00:10:02.220
report it to the application owner,
and don't fix it in the lab.

148
00:10:05.970 --> 00:10:11.025
The federal requirement, from NIST special
publication eight hundred fifty-three,

149
00:10:11.025 --> 00:10:14.395
revision five, as the guide's
requirements table maps it.

150
00:10:14.395 --> 00:10:18.872
Step 3 supports S C five,
denial-of-service protection, and S I four,

151
00:10:18.872 --> 00:10:24.616
system monitoring, aligned with the fed-ramp
twenty X key security indicators the

152
00:10:24.616 --> 00:10:27.488
guide names; the evidence is the coverage,

153
00:10:27.488 --> 00:10:30.782
the metrics and logs, and
the simulation report.

154
00:10:30.782 --> 00:10:33.854
The guide labels the
control itself Mandated,

155
00:10:33.854 --> 00:10:39.349
because S C five is in the Moderate baseline
that FIPS two hundred requires, and the

156
00:10:39.349 --> 00:10:44.683
way to meet it, DDoS Protection on every
public I P, as Microsoft's recommendation.

157
00:10:44.683 --> 00:10:50.449
Step 4 supports S C seven, boundary
protection, and S I ten, information input

158
00:10:50.449 --> 00:10:54.075
validation; its evidence
is the policy exports,

159
00:10:54.075 --> 00:10:56.958
the logs and the blocked test request.

160
00:10:56.958 --> 00:10:59.034
The steps support these controls,

161
00:10:59.034 --> 00:11:02.570
and the assessor decides
whether each is satisfied.

162
00:11:06.320 --> 00:11:08.295
Check yourself with two questions.

163
00:11:08.295 --> 00:11:12.245
First: a public I P is
attached to a NAT gateway.

164
00:11:12.245 --> 00:11:14.732
What does DDoS Network
Protection do for it?

165
00:11:14.732 --> 00:11:16.620
The answer is b, nothing.

166
00:11:16.620 --> 00:11:20.860
Learn's tier comparison says NAT
gateway I Ps aren't supported,

167
00:11:20.860 --> 00:11:23.520
so the guide lists them as an exclusion.

168
00:11:23.520 --> 00:11:27.495
Second: a web application
firewall policy shows Detection.

169
00:11:27.495 --> 00:11:29.545
What happens to an attack request?

170
00:11:29.545 --> 00:11:33.170
The answer is b: it's logged
and forwarded to the backend.

171
00:11:33.170 --> 00:11:35.057
Only prevention mode blocks.

172
00:11:35.057 --> 00:11:40.340
The module page has three more questions,
each linked to the unit that answers it.

173
00:11:44.090 --> 00:11:44.915
To sum up.

174
00:11:44.915 --> 00:11:48.203
You chose a DDoS tier and
named what it doesn't cover.

175
00:11:48.203 --> 00:11:52.764
You checked that every public I P
is protected, logged and alerting.

176
00:11:52.764 --> 00:11:57.577
You took the web application firewall from
detection to prevention, tuned first.

177
00:11:57.577 --> 00:12:01.889
And you proved it blocks a test request,
with the matching row in its log.

178
00:12:01.889 --> 00:12:07.226
The evidence an I S S O keeps from this
module: the plan and its linked networks,

179
00:12:07.226 --> 00:12:11.292
the per-I P mode export, DDoS
metrics, logs and alert history,

180
00:12:11.292 --> 00:12:17.136
the simulation report when the exercise
runs, the firewall policy exports and logs,

181
00:12:17.136 --> 00:12:21.202
the blocked test request, and
the rule-exception register.

182
00:12:21.202 --> 00:12:23.962
Next is Module 3, the edge and the egress:

183
00:12:23.962 --> 00:12:29.810
an origin nothing can reach around Front
Door, and egress through Azure Firewall.
